    "q-105596": {
      "dom_id": "step-q105596",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 & 6 \\\\ -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -10 & 6 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\boxed{} & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -14\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\n$$\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. What is the value of the entry ${l}_{21}$ in the matrix $L?$",
      "worked_solution": "We want to make $0$ at the position ${a}_{21}$ using Gaussian elimination. To do this, we take the $2$nd row of $A,$ and we add the $1$st row of $A$ multiplied by $:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 & 6 \\\\ -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -10 & 6 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 12 \\\\ -10 & 6 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe entry ${l}_{21}$ of the matrix $L$ is the factor next to ${R}_{1},$ taken with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n{l}_{21}=-(2)=-2.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
